From: y52s recovery work
Subject: [PATCH] y52s: cache the synthetic password, not the key derived from it

Run 42948073811 proved 0015 changed nothing:

  y52s: sid token: gk input 32 bytes (v3)                       <- hex decoded fine
  y52s: gk verify .../0/spblob/0000000000000000.handle (uid 0):
        sid=0x779b8423e32ee87 call_ok=1 code=-1 timeout=0 token=0

call_ok=1 with the right handle, the right uid and a 32-byte secret means the
HAL was reached and rejected the secret.  So the secret was wrong, and the
reason is embarrassingly simple: we were not hashing the synthetic password at
all.

unwrapSyntheticPasswordBlob() returns

  disk_decryption_secret_key = PersonalizedHashSP800(PERSONALIZATION_FBE_KEY,
                                      PERSONALISATION_CONTEXT, secret_key, ...)

i.e. the FBE *disk* key derived from the SP, and that is what
Decrypt_User_Synth_Pass() assigns to `secret` and hands to
Decrypt_CE_storage().  The synthetic password itself is the intermediate
plaintext `secret_key` inside the unwrap -- it is consumed right there and
never leaves the function.

deriveGkPassword() hashes the SP, so feeding it the disk key is a completely
different input: same handle, same uid, wrong key material, ERROR_INVALID
every time.  Nothing about the tag mask (0014) or the digest encoding (0015)
was wrong; they were both necessary and both insufficient.

Fix: capture `secret_key` at the one point where the SP exists in the clear,
and adopt it in Decrypt_User_Synth_Pass() instead of the return value.  The
bytes are taken verbatim and their length is whatever the blob holds (the
framework stores the SP as a hex String, so `getBytes()` is those ASCII
characters -- re-encoding would change the key).  The first user to unwrap
wins, so an app clone's own SP cannot overwrite the parent's.

Expected trace:

  y52s: synthetic password captured (128 bytes, v3)
  y52s: synthetic password cached for user 0 (128 bytes, v3)
  y52s: sid token: gk input 32 bytes (v3)
  y52s: gk verify ... (uid 0): call_ok=1 code=0 timeout=0 token=115
  y52s: tied-profile credential recovered (80 bytes)

---
--- a/Decrypt.cpp
+++ b/Decrypt.cpp
@@ -470,6 +470,16 @@
 	return found_subid;
 }
 
+/* y52s: the synthetic password, captured at the only place it exists in the
+ * clear -- inside unwrapSyntheticPasswordBlob(), between the AES-GCM open of
+ * the payload and the FBE key derivation that consumes it.  The value that
+ * function RETURNS is the disk key, and gatekeeper enrolments know nothing
+ * about it; caching the return value as "the SP" is what made every SID
+ * token come back as gatekeeper code -1 (ERROR_INVALID) with the handle and
+ * the uid both correct. */
+static std::string g_y52s_sp_capture;
+static bool g_y52s_sp_capture_v3 = true;
+
 /* C++ replacement for function of the same name using direct Keymaster HIDL
  * https://android.googlesource.com/platform/frameworks/base/+/android-8.0.0_r23/services/core/java/com/android/server/locksettings/SyntheticPasswordManager.java#867
  * returning an empty string indicates an error */
@@ -878,6 +888,20 @@
 			return disk_decryption_secret_key;
 		}
 
+		/* y52s: this plaintext IS the synthetic password.  Everything below
+		 * turns it into the disk key, and AOSP's deriveGkPassword() hashes
+		 * the SP -- not the disk key -- under "sp-gk-authentication".
+		 * Take the bytes verbatim: the framework keeps the SP as a hex
+		 * String (`syntheticPassword.getBytes()`), so its ASCII case is
+		 * already what AOSP feeds to HMAC; re-encoding it would change the
+		 * key.  Length is whatever the blob holds (128 chars for SHA-512
+		 * based SPs), never assumed. */
+		g_y52s_sp_capture.assign((const char*)secret_key, secret_key_real_size);
+		g_y52s_sp_capture_v3 =
+				(*synthetic_password_version == SYNTHETIC_PASSWORD_VERSION_V3);
+		printf("y52s: synthetic password captured (%zu bytes, v%d)\n",
+		       g_y52s_sp_capture.size(), g_y52s_sp_capture_v3 ? 3 : 2);
+
 		// Generate disk decryption key
 		if (*synthetic_password_version == SYNTHETIC_PASSWORD_VERSION_V3) {
 			disk_decryption_secret_key = PersonalizedHashSP800(PERSONALIZATION_FBE_KEY,
@@ -1800,19 +1824,21 @@
 
 	/* y52s: keep the synthetic password so the SID enrolment can be opened
 	 * with it (AOSP deriveGkPassword()), and so a tied profile unlocked in
-	 * the same run does not need a second look at /data.  The SP version
-	 * decides which KDF to use, and it is read from the blob itself. */
+	 * the same run does not need a second look at /data.
+	 *
+	 * `secret` above is NOT the SP: unwrapSyntheticPasswordBlob() returns the
+	 * disk key it derived from the SP.  The SP itself was captured inside
+	 * that function, before the derivation, and is adopted here.  The first
+	 * user to unwrap wins, so a later tier (an app clone's own SP) cannot
+	 * overwrite the parent's. */
 	{
-		g_y52s_sp = secret;
-		g_y52s_sp_user = user_id;
-		std::string version_blob;
-		if (android::base::ReadFileToString(
-				spblob_path + handle_str + ".spblob", &version_blob) &&
-		    !version_blob.empty())
-			g_y52s_sp_v3 = ((uint8_t)version_blob[0] ==
-					SYNTHETIC_PASSWORD_VERSION_V3);
-		printf("y52s: synthetic password cached for user %d (v%d)\n",
-		       (int)user_id, g_y52s_sp_v3 ? 3 : 2);
+		if (g_y52s_sp.empty() || g_y52s_sp_user == user_id) {
+			g_y52s_sp = g_y52s_sp_capture;
+			g_y52s_sp_v3 = g_y52s_sp_capture_v3;
+			g_y52s_sp_user = user_id;
+		}
+		printf("y52s: synthetic password cached for user %d (%zu bytes, v%d)\n",
+		       (int)user_id, g_y52s_sp.size(), g_y52s_sp_v3 ? 3 : 2);
 	}
 
 	/* y52s: now that this user's synthetic password is in hand, recover every
